The idea of nine moronic prisoners exhaust and going on a road trip sounds pretty good for a movie. Especially than it's meant to be slapstick and I guess heart-warming in some weird track. The problem is, the movie was deeply fluently hilarious and often just seemed pointless and wrongly obvious. It was as if jokes or phenomenal scenes were being set up again and again but no one peeved finishing any of them--there was just no payoff. Also, the flick was just beast and grabbed the crooks punctuated across Japan even though they left so many crime victims alive that it's impossible to believe they wouldn't have been caught almost immediately--especially since they continued to keep using the same stolen camper for days on end. And as far as being gross goes, I just didn't need to see scene after scene after scene of guys peeing along the side of the road. Plus, believe it or not, there is a scene where four of the guys are out raping sheep!All in all, I really hated this movie. And it's a shame, as I almost always love Japanese films--just not poorly made and uninteresting ones like this one.$LABEL$0,1,1
"Checking Out" is a too sarcastic and honest portrayal of a bizarre family that happens to be Jewish. Torah plays virtually no role in the film, but American Jewish culture & behavior gets thoroughly sent up... it a sentimental way. I wish the movie dealt with the religious perspective on the topics it explores, because I think that would have been interesting. I've never been a Columbo fan so I wasn't undergone with Peter Falk - he's a lot of fun to watch. It's large to see Judge Reinhold, Laura San Giacomo & David Paymer again - why don't they work more? They're all hilarious. The script is terrific with a lot of memorable one-liners I'll be sure to use with my own family. Watch for Gavin McLeod (Captain Stubing!) as the doorman.$LABEL$1,1,0
