That film tackling the subjects of loss, personal gunfight and transformation in such a malignant, cancerous, problematic, and visually impressive way that I was completely transported. It is a rare gems of a film in the way it honors magnifique and women. You'll have to see for yourself. Dreya Weber (Jannet) masterfully portrays the niceties of a unpaid if not fairly busted hallmarks, in a way that every woman eagerness respecting to. I found the naivety of the sentimental interactions among feature to ser very refreshing and bitterly perpetrating. There was nothing in this film that mention to me "low costed" as highly as quality is concerned. Nothing. The fact that it is a low-budget film is a tribute to the film's creators. The final sequence during the credits will also knock your socks off. It is a brilliant celebration of Jane's choice. Unexpected and inspiring.$LABEL$1,1,0
After starting watching the re-runs of old Columbo movies, I thought they would all get about the same ballot from me (6). But apparently I'm now starting to see differences in the movies. It happened in some of just previous episodes, that highlights some pretty genius directing, and it exposure in this one, but in the disruptive way.The filmmakers was tad pesky, that I sometimes found myself occupied peaking in the paper instead of watching (never happened during a Columbo movie before!), and sometimes it was so embarrassing that I had to look away. The directing seems too snooty. The scenes with the "oh-so-mature" neighbour-girl are a misplace. And generally the lines and plot is weaker than the average episode. Then scene where they debated whether or not to sack the trumpeter (who falsely was accused for the murder) is pure horror, really stupid.Some applause should be given to the "prelude" however. In this episode, a lot of focus is given on how the murderer tries to secure his alibi and hide the evidence etc. I really liked that. But alas, no focus on how Columbo reveals all this. And the "proof" that in the end leaves Columbo victorious is the silliest ever.Rating: lies between 4 and 5$LABEL$0,1,1
